---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<Base
  title={`Privacy · ${site.name}`}
  description={`What ${site.name} collects, and what it doesn’t.`}
>
  <article class="container prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated November 2026</p>
    <p>
      {site.name} is built so that we learn as little about you as possible. There are no accounts,
      no analytics and no advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>This website and the web app</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No analytics, no tracking cookies and no third-party scripts. Fonts are served from our own
        domain.
      </li>
      <li>
        Our host, Cloudflare, processes standard request data such as IP address and browser to
        deliver and protect the service. We don’t use it to build profiles.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The app</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your library, progress, downloads and playlists are stored on your device.</li>
      <li>
        If you turn on sync, your data is encrypted on your device before it is sent. Our sync
        server stores only that encrypted data and can’t read it. Removing sync from your devices
        stops it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Our sync server deletes a sync group when none of its devices has sent changes for 2 years.
        Your devices keep their data; you can start sync again. A server you host yourself keeps
        groups unless you set a time.
      </li>
      <li>
        On Android, the sync key and the passwords for your own servers are encrypted with a key
        kept in the phone’s Android Keystore, and they are left out of backups. After restoring a
        backup on a new phone, you pair sync and enter those passwords again.
      </li>
      <li>
        Audio is fetched directly from the sources your add-ons point to. It never passes through
        our servers.
      </li>
      <li>
        The diagnostics log in Settings is off until you turn it on. It stays on your device, holds
        no titles, file names, addresses or logins, and the app never sends it anywhere: it only
        leaves when you share the file yourself, for example with a bug report.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Add-ons</h2>
    <p>
      Add-ons are run by whoever makes them. When the app talks to an add-on, that add-on’s own
      privacy practices apply. Our own add-ons log only what is needed to run them and keep no
      listening history.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about privacy: <a href={`mailto:${site.privacyEmail}`}>{site.privacyEmail}</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
